<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zen Buddhism, Chan Buddhism, Seon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zen is a school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a> that stresses meditation and direct insight over scripture. It arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> as Chan, spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> as Seon and was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> by Eisai and Dogen in the late 12th and 13th centuries. Patronised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> and Ashikaga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunates</a>, Zen temples shaped Japanese ink painting, gardens, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony">tea ceremony</a> and Noh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zen.jpg" alt="Zen" width="320" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christopher J. Fynn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashtamangala_Lotus.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word Zen comes from the Japanese pronunciation of the Middle Chinese word for meditation, itself derived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> dhyāna, which can be rendered as contemplation or a meditative state. The tradition developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> by blending Indian Mahayana Buddhism, particularly the Yogachara and Madhyamaka philosophies, with Chinese Taoist thought. It began as the Chan school, also called the Buddha-mind school. Tradition holds that it was brought to China by the monk Bodhidharma, an Indian or Central Asian figure.</p><p>Meditation practice first entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> through the translations of An Shigao and Kumārajīva (334–413 CE), who rendered dhyāna sutras based on the teaching of the Kashmiri Sarvāstivāda school. By the time of the Fifth Patriarch Hongren (601–674) the school was established as a separate school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. Chan later spread south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> as Thiền, northeast to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> as Seon and east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> as Zen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Zen emphasises meditation, especially sitting meditation known as zazen in Japanese, and direct insight into one's own Buddha nature, called kenshō in Japanese. Its doctrine draws on the bodhisattva path, Madhyamaka, Yogachara, the Prajñāpāramitā literature and Buddha-nature texts such as the Laṅkāvatāra Sūtra. Some sources describe it as a special transmission outside scriptures, yet masters such as Guifeng Zongmi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinul">Jinul</a> argued for the unity of Zen and the Buddhist teachings, and most Zen schools also keep chanting, precepts, ritual and scriptural study.</p><p>Two main lineages exist today: the Caodong tradition, traced to Dongshan Liangjie (807–869), and the Linji school, traced to Linji Yixuan, who died in 866. In the Song dynasty Caodong became associated with silent illumination, formulated by Hongzhi Zhengjue (1091–1157), while Linji became associated with Dahui Zonggao (1089–1163) and meditation on the huatou, the critical phrase of a koan. Teachers pass on lineages by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a> transmission from master to disciple.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Zen developed in a monastic setting, and most masters have been ordained monastics. East Asian monasteries stress self-sufficiency, so Zen monks do not live by begging but cook their own food and may farm. Japanese monks and nuns can marry after ordination because they ordain under the bodhisattva vows rather than the traditional monastic code. Intensive retreats called sesshin combine long sitting periods with formal meals and short periods of work.</p><p>Zen has also used the arts as training. Chan monk painters such as Guanxiu and Muqi Fachang communicated their understanding through brush painting and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>, and calligraphy, poetry, gardening, flower arrangement and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony">tea ceremony</a> have been used in practice. The Shaolin Monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> developed the first institutionalised form of gōngfu. The scholar Robert Sharf argues that seated meditation was less central to historical Zen monastery life than is often thought.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Great monasteries owned land and slaves, the monk Uicheon founded the Cheontae school, Jinul reformed <b>Seon</b> (Zen) Buddhism, and the state festivals of Yeondeunghoe and Palgwanhoe combined Buddhist ritual with native cults.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Over the centuries Chinese Buddhism developed schools of its own, among them Chan, better known by its Japanese name, <b>Zen</b>, which travelled onward to Korea, Japan, and Vietnam.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
